    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Large hydraulic fracturing operations have become commonplace, creating a need for better understanding of the flow near fractured wells. The expense of these operations has also increased the importance of diagnostic tools for analyzing the resulting fracture. The type-curve matching technique of analyzing transient pressure test data can yield estimates of fracture length, based on the common assumption that hydraulic fractures have effectively infinite permeability. However, a number of recant field tests have produced apparent fracture lengths far smaller than would be expected on the basis of both design calculations and knowledge of treatment volumes. One hypothesis advanced to explain this behavior is the existence of finite conductivity within the fracture, causing a deviation from ideal behavior. A finite element numerical model was devised to analyze the problem of Darcy flow in two regions of differing permeability and porosity. The well and fracture were presumed to fully penetrate the height of the formation, creating a two-dimensional flow field. Galerkin's criterion, in conjunction with interpolation functions giving pressure continuity, was used to produce approximate solutions to the diffusivity equation. This model has demonstrated very good agreement with analytic results available for special cases. The calculations reveal that the early time behavior of a well after a change in production rate can be described solely in terms of a relative conductance parameter consisting of the ratio of fracture to formation permeability divided by the ratio of fracture length to fracture width. This parameter has been used to construct type curves for use in analyzing tests of wells having finite-conductivity vertical fractures in both closed and unbounded reservoirs. The results of calculations made for fractures with greatly varied conductivities show that potentiometric model studies of this problem are not reliable. Design charts based on experimental data published in the literature should be revised. It is concluded that the existance of even modest impairment of fracture conductivity can cause a substantial reduction in the apparent fracture length computed from a transient well test if the analysis is based on the assumption of infinite fracture conductivity. To correct this problem, new type curves are presented. It is further demonstrated that the combination of type curve matching with conventional analysis methods can yield results having a high degree of credibility.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
